<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huna people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hunas, Huna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huna were Central Asian invaders, related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, who pressed into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the mid-fifth century CE. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toramana">Toramana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> they occupied large parts of north-western India. Their invasions strained Gupta finances and loyalties and were a major cause of the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Huna was the name ancient Indians gave to several Central Asian tribes who came into the subcontinent by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>. It covered the Kidarites, or Red Huns, and the Alchon Huns, and was also used for the Nezak Huns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, or White Huns, who did not enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. These groups were not one people, though the name refers to them collectively. Chinese sources link them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and to the Huns who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Gerald Larson suggests they were a Turkic-Mongolic grouping; their relation to the European Huns remains disputed. Their original beliefs are unknown and are thought to have combined ancestor worship, totemism and animism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Kidarites, who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> in the second half of the 4th century, are generally regarded as the first wave of Hunas, and set up principalities in Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The Alchon Huns later defeated them in the Kabul Valley. Skandagupta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> repulsed an Alchon attack on the north-west in 460 CE, but after his death the Alchons under Khingila I took the Kidarite principalities and from there fanned out into northern, western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>At their farthest the Huna lands reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and they occupied places as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eran">Eran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kausambi">Kosambi</a>. A coalition of Indian princes, among them King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodharman">Yashodharman</a> and the Gupta emperor Narasimhagupta, defeated the Alchon army and its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> in 528 CE and drove them out of India; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> are thought to have played only a minor part in the campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The victory of 528 was recorded on a stone pillar set up at Mandasaur in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodharman">Yashodharman</a>. The Hunas also appear in ancient Indian literature, including the Rāmāyaṇa, the Mahābhārata, the Purāṇas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>'s Raghuvaṃśa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huna people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gupta decline began with the <b>Huna</b> invasions of the later fifth century CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Devapala’s inscriptions claim victories over the Utkalas of Orissa, the <b>Hunas</b>, the Dravidas and the rulers of Pragjyotisha in Assam, claims that are exaggerated but suggest real campaigns to the south and east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1930s the American author Max Freedom Long originated a philosophy and practice he called <b>Huna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hawaiian-religion">Hawaiian religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…CE, with the oldest preserved parts not much older than around 400 BCE, and the text probably reached its final form by the early Gupta period (c. 4th century CE); a mention of the <b>Huna</b> implies that the Bhishma Parva may have been edited around the 4th century.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Guptas, who favoured Brahmins but tolerated other faiths, great monasteries such as Nalanda became centres of learning, though the <b>Huna</b> king Mihirakula destroyed many others in the early 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
